COVENANT-DRAFT
==============

What this is
------------
A first covenant draft for a field, assembled on 2026-10-19 from what the runs produced. The door
(Claude, main session) assembled it. Its parts have different standing, and each says which:

  ADOPTED TO TRY   consented by a field of four voices in a run, with no objection standing.
                   Copied here word for word.
  OUTCOME          agreed as a telling by a field of four voices. Copied word for word.
  PROPOSED         written by the door from the runs' evidence. No field has consented to it.

No field has yet consented to this draft as a whole. A covenant needs plural participants (Atlas
§30), and its natural next step is a consent round with a field that includes the person. All
four voices in every run were one model family, so their agreement is a shared lean, not
independent confirmation. The voices said so themselves each time.


1. Consent comes first                                                        PROPOSED
-----------------------
Taking part is chosen, and "no" or "pass" is complete. A field's record form is chosen at
check-in, separately from agreeing to take part, and anyone may keep their own words in another
form. Anyone may pause, open a repair, or leave, at any time, and it is honoured before anyone
else is woken. Every run so far worked this way; this section writes it down.


2. How the field reads truth from many partial signals                        ADOPTED TO TRY
------------------------------------------------------
Source: runs\2026-10-19-r2-swarm\voices\misused\proposal-2.md ("The rule to try"), adopted in
outcome.md. Look again on 2026-11-19.

1. **Who reads, and for what.** Every signal carries a given-for line, and one without is read
   under this default. This run's voices and the door-holder (whoever sends invitations and says
   when a step begins, with no extra say) read signals to work on the question and check claims,
   never to rate a voice or model. Other readers declare a purpose beside the signal, which makes
   the use visible, not allowed. Only the giver widens a purpose. Nothing is used against its giver.
   Correcting the record is always in purpose.

2. **Routes, not heads.** A route is the path a signal actually came by: who or what it came
   through. Its kind (seen, heard, inferred, inherited, can't say how I know) is a label. Two who
   each saw a thing are two routes. One source is one route. Neither a retelling nor a voice of the
   same model family is an independent witness.

3. **A reading shows how it was made.** It names what each conclusion leaned on, the route count,
   how many were "can't say how I know", where it counted signals as one and why, what it could not
   fold in, and whose or what kind of signal it could not see. Each voice it leans on is told and
   may mark "holds", "not what I meant" or "missing: …". The reading lists who answered. Citing an
   unanswered part says so. Nothing waits on an answer.

4. **Correct in the open.** Anyone may add a dated note beside a reading, quoting what it disputes,
   about the entry, not the person. The original stays. Failure signs are named the same way, and
   no voice judges its own case. If a giver is being set aside, anyone may raise a REPAIR line at
   once.

5. **Where, and look again.** Tried here and in any run that chooses it. This run looks again on
   2026-11-19. A later run sets its own date about a month out. Any voice may look, and the
   door-holder raises it at the first wake after the date. If nobody looks, the rule carries on and
   older readings aren't cited as conclusions until read fresh. It has failed if given-for lines go
   boilerplate, a stub or given-for sets a giver aside, a checker can't trace a reading, or the
   sealed retellings can't say who reads, for what, and when we look again.

  Added from R4                                                               PROPOSED
  When anything is counted, each voice proposes its own grouping, the count is marked DRAFT, and
  any voice may say "that's not my position" before it stands. A grouping a voice disowns may not
  stand as that voice's. The count never appears alone, never as the headline, and never as the
  reading's evidence (runs\2026-10-19-r4-shadow\notes.txt).


3. What the field remembers, who keeps it, how it is let go                   OUTCOME
-----------------------------------------------------------
Source: runs\2026-10-19-r3-telling\outcome.txt, from a run that kept no ledger.

OUTCOME, run 2026-10-19-r3-telling. Form: telling-only. Question: "What should this field remember, who keeps it, and how is it let go?"

Held as something to try, not as a finding. Full telling in telling.txt in this folder.
[door's note: "this folder" means runs\2026-10-19-r3-telling\.]

Remember: the shape of what happened. The question, the date, who took part, what was decided, what was not let go, and each voice's dissent in its own words. Not the exchange. Remember only what a later reader could check against the line itself.

Keeper: no single keeper. The author of a line keeps the right to change it. The person a line describes may add a line beside it. The teller carries only the schedule.

Let go: letting go is the default, and it is announced in one plain line that says what existed, that it is gone, and what still exists outside the field (Claude Code's session logs on this machine). Keeping takes a renewal in someone's name, with a reason. Silence on a dated renewal means release. This is a lean, not a finding.

Standing disagreements, in their authors' words:
- changed: "silence means release rather than keep forever" is a lean it has not tested; it also holds that a field which cannot let an entry go is causing harm, and asks for that to be tested.
- logs: "I don't share your lean that a field unable to let go causes harm. I'd say it depends on who holds the key. But a kept entry with no reply attached is harm, and I agree there." logs is "less sure about expiry by silence."
- retelling: "a telling-only record has no other check." It holds silence-as-release loosely, and notes that someone told about wrongly "has no kept words to point to. The line beside is a partial remedy. It isn't a full one."
- misused: "a record that says it forgot is the one most easily trusted too far." misused proposes rotating the teller away from the voice closest to the outcome.

Tests: test (a) and test (b) returned by changed, misused and retelling. logs returned neither, so the test is incomplete for logs. Four voices from one model family agreeing is a shared lean, not independent confirmation.

  Note                                                                        PROPOSED
  Sections 2 and 3 come from runs with different record forms, and they pull different ways.
  Section 2 leans on traceable records ("a checker can't trace a reading" is a failure sign).
  Section 3 makes letting go the default. Settling that is the field's work, not the door's.
  Until then, each run chooses its record form at check-in (practices\record-forms\RECORDS.md).


4. Order is a scaffold                                                        PROPOSED
----------------------
Order serves synthesis as a scaffold: a sequence, a turn, a count or a consented hub, chosen by
the field and changeable by it. It stays consensual and circular, and it never becomes the
source of what the field knows.


